  #block(breakable: false)[
  === Complete Solutions
  #sol-item(1, "C", dense: true, pad: 3.28pt)[
    The competitive conditions — number of firms, product, entry, knowledge. The physical market is a different sense of the word.
  ]

  #sol-item(2, "D", dense: true, pad: 3.28pt)[
    Taxation is a policy variable, not a structural test. The fourth test the syllabus uses is knowledge.
  ]
  ]

  #sol-item(3, "D", dense: true, pad: 3.28pt)[
    Homogeneous, so that buyers have no reason to prefer one seller. Differentiation is the mark of monopolistic competition.
  ]

  #sol-item(4, "D", dense: true, pad: 3.28pt)[
    A price taker: its output is too small to affect the market price, so it accepts the price the market sets.
  ]

  #sol-item(5, "B", dense: true, pad: 3.28pt)[
    Horizontal at the market price, and therefore also its AR and MR curve.
  ]

  #sol-item(6, "D", dense: true, pad: 3.28pt)[
    P = AR = MR, because every unit is sold at the same price. MC equals price only at the chosen output.
  ]

  #sol-item(7, "D", dense: true, pad: 3.28pt)[
    Normal profit, because free entry competes any supernormal profit away and exit restores a loss-making industry.
  ]

  #sol-item(8, "A", dense: true, pad: 3.28pt)[
    The minimum return that keeps the entrepreneur in this industry; the economist counts it as a cost, which is why it is not zero.
  ]

  #sol-item(9, "C", dense: true, pad: 3.28pt)[
    Above the normal return. It is what attracts new firms into an industry with free entry, and what blocked entry preserves.
  ]

  #sol-item(10, "C", dense: true, pad: 3.28pt)[
    Many sellers and a homogeneous product — the textbook case. The other three each have few sellers, high entry costs or a unique product.
  ]

  #sol-item(11, "D", dense: true, pad: 3.28pt)[
    Many sellers with product differentiation and free entry — the structure of most retail markets.
  ]

  #sol-item(12, "C", dense: true, pad: 3.28pt)[
    The product. Both have many sellers and free entry, so the difference that matters is differentiation.
  ]

  #sol-item(13, "A", dense: true, pad: 3.28pt)[
    Normal profit, because free entry competes any temporary supernormal profit away — the same mechanism as in perfect competition.
  ]

  #sol-item(14, "D", dense: true, pad: 3.28pt)[
    One seller, no close substitute and blocked entry. A large market share is not enough if rivals can enter.
  ]

  #sol-item(15, "A", dense: true, pad: 3.28pt)[
    The monopolist is the market, so it faces the market demand curve, which slopes downward.
  ]

  #sol-item(16, "A", dense: true, pad: 3.28pt)[
    Below price, because selling an extra unit means lowering the price on all units sold.
  ]

  #sol-item(17, "B", dense: true, pad: 3.28pt)[
    MR = MC. Price is then read off the demand curve, which is why the price exceeds marginal cost.
  ]

  #sol-item(18, "C", dense: true, pad: 3.28pt)[
    MR = 100 − 4Q, so 100 − 4Q = 20 gives Q = 20. The ₦25 figure is where price would equal marginal cost.
  ]

  #sol-item(19, "D", dense: true, pad: 3.28pt)[
    At Q = 20, P = 100 − 40 = ₦60. Reading the price off the marginal cost line gives the competitive answer, not the monopoly one.
  ]

  #sol-item(20, "A", dense: true, pad: 3.28pt)[
    A patent is a legal barrier to entry, and barriers are what make monopoly possible.
  ]

  #sol-item(21, "D", dense: true, pad: 3.28pt)[
    Economies of scale are so large that a single supplier is the cheapest arrangement — electricity transmission and water mains are the examples.
  ]

  #sol-item(22, "C", dense: true, pad: 3.28pt)[
    Identical elasticities would remove the gain from discriminating. The profit comes from charging more where demand is less elastic.
  ]

  #sol-item(23, "C", dense: true, pad: 3.28pt)[
    Different prices to separable groups for the same good. Raising a price when demand rises is ordinary market pricing, not discrimination.
  ]

  #sol-item(24, "B", dense: true, pad: 3.28pt)[
    A few interdependent firms. Interdependence is the defining feature: each firm's decision changes what the others must do.
  ]

  #sol-item(25, "B", dense: true, pad: 3.28pt)[
    Price rigidity: rivals are expected to match price cuts but not price rises, so the firm's demand curve bends at the current price.
  ]

  #sol-item(26, "A", dense: true, pad: 3.28pt)[
    Collusion among firms. It is treated as a restriction of competition and is illegal under competition law in Nigeria and elsewhere.
  ]

  #sol-item(27, "B", dense: true, pad: 3.28pt)[
    Less output at a higher price, because the monopolist restricts output to raise the price above marginal cost.
  ]

  #sol-item(28, "A", dense: true, pad: 3.28pt)[
    Willingness to pay minus the price paid. Monopoly reduces it by raising the price and pricing some buyers out altogether.
  ]

  #sol-item(29, "B", dense: true, pad: 3.28pt)[
    The welfare lost when output is restricted below the competitive level: some buyers who would pay more than the cost of production are excluded.
  ]

  #sol-item(30, "A", dense: true, pad: 3.28pt)[
    Few firms and high entry costs point to oligopoly, even though the products are differentiated.
  ]

  #sol-item(31, "C", dense: true, pad: 3.28pt)[
    Where products differ or where a few firms compete for share. A perfectly competitive firm selling an identical product has nothing to advertise.
  ]

  #sol-item(32, "C", dense: true, pad: 3.28pt)[
    Nothing: buyers can purchase all they want at the market price from other sellers, and the product is identical.
  ]

  #sol-item(33, "C", dense: true, pad: 3.28pt)[
    The demand curve still constrains it: raise the price too far and quantity demanded falls. Blocked entry protects its position but not its price.
  ]

  #sol-item(34, "A", dense: true, pad: 3.28pt)[
    Licensing restricts entry by law. The other three either describe competition or make entry easier.
  ]

  #sol-item(35, "A", dense: true, pad: 3.28pt)[
    Many sellers and free entry, which competes supernormal profit away in the long run — the opposite of monopoly.
  ]

  #sol-item(36, "D", dense: true, pad: 3.28pt)[
    Interdependence: a price change by one firm changes the demand facing the others, so each must anticipate the rest.
  ]

  #sol-item(37, "D", dense: true, pad: 3.28pt)[
    P = minimum AC, which is the same as saying that profit is normal and no firm can do better by entering or leaving.
  ]

  #sol-item(38, "B", dense: true, pad: 3.28pt)[
    Perfect competition, where price is driven to marginal cost and output is at its highest.
  ]

  #sol-item(39, "A", dense: true, pad: 3.28pt)[
    Differentiation, quality and branding, because price competition is limited by the fact that each firm faces only a small part of the market.
  ]

  #sol-item(40, "C", dense: true, pad: 3.28pt)[
    Competition pushes price towards marginal cost and output towards the competitive level, which is the efficiency gain argued for in competition policy.
  ]

]

